## Observations

First off, there is clearly a wide-variety of SDGs that the domain industry is working towards. In fact, all but one of the SDGs is accounted for by at least one company.

<div class="news-img-40pct">

![13. Climate action](/img/sdg/13-climate-action.svg)

</div>

By far, the most commonly supported SDG is **Climate Action**. This is hardly surprising given that global climate change is one of the main existential risks facing humanity. The emissions associated with the energy used to operate and power the Internet is a key contributor to global warming. Hence, the domain industry has a role to play in mitigating climate change by ensuring that our services are powered by renewable energy wherever possible.

<div class="news-img-40pct">

![8. Decent work](/img/sdg/8-work.svg)

</div>

The next most popular SDG is **Decent Work and Economic Growth**. It perhaps expected that technology companies would prioritize this SDG. Often technology companies prioritize the quality of the work environment to attract and retain talented and experienced staff in a competitive market. This SDG is also about driving sustainable economic growth, building companies intended to withstand shocks and last long-term.

<div class="news-img-40pct">

![7. Clean energy](/img/sdg/7-energy.svg)

</div>

It is somewhat surprising that this SDG did not rank higher given the emphasis on Climate Action. One key way that the domain industry can decarbonize the Internet is by only procuring renewable energy to power data centres and maximizing the energy efficiency of technical infrastructure. The lower popularity of this SDG is potentially an area for investment and focus.

<div class="news-img-40pct">

![9. Innovation](/img/sdg/9-industry.svg)

</div>

The Internet has become a piece of critical infrastructure that supports the efficient operation of modern societies. Domain names are a cornerstone of this, so it makes sense that this SDG is an area of focus. Many of the companies in the domain industry are also focused on innovation to support the inclusive reach of these technologies by working to make the Internet universally accessible.

<div class="news-img-40pct">

![12. Responsible consumption](/img/sdg/12-consumption-production.svg)

</div>

As a virtual good, domain names are responsibly produced, consumed and disposed of, so it is clear why many in the domain industry support this SDG. This SDG also entails a commitment to regularly sustainability reporting which many of these companies do.

## Next steps

If you are a company or an organization that is looking to get started with the SDGs, we recommend learning more about how you can [align with the SDGs](/news/aligning-with-the-sdgs/) and reading our guidance about [finding your environmental and social purpose](https://www.godaddy.com/resources/news/finding-the-environmental-or-social-purpose-for-your-business) on the GoDaddy blog.

If you are a domain retailer and are considering adopting the SDGs, we hope this post provides you with some insight into which SDGs others in our industry are supporting. Once you have published your SDG commitments, please <a href="#" class="chat-link">let us know</a> &mdash; we'd be happy to include your in this post.

And if you are interested to better understand how the .eco community supports the Sustainable Development Goals, we recommend checking out the [.eco profile system](https://profiles.eco/) where you can see the sustainability commitments made for every active .eco domain.

